





           
                     D B D E S I G N 
                     by Dave Moorman 
           
           
              The major purpose of DBDesign is to produce FTS screens. An 

          FTS file contains Font/Screen/Color data, and can be bloaded 
          and used quickly with DotBASIC Plus. With DBD, you can visually 

          design your user interface, which will snap up on the screen in 

          an instant! 
           
           
           WHAT YOU CAN DO 
           --------------- 
              The opening screen is blank, with the menu bar on the top 
          line. You can remove the menu bar by pressing <F1>. To turn on 
          the Font/Color window, press <F3>. Press <F5> to toggle between 

          Font and Color windows. And, pressing <UP ARROW> toggles the 
          window between the top and bottom of the screen. The menu bar 
          and window do not affect your screen. 
           
              So press <F3> and <F5> until you have the Font window 
          visible. Move the arrow to a character on the font and click. 
          The character is "picked up". Move it to any place on the open 
          screen and click to "place" it. Right click will make the 
          character go away without placing it. 
           
              If the picked up character is a SPACE (chr$(32)), a color 
          square appears. This just makes it easier to see where it is. 
           
              You can pick up a character off the screen as well. And, if 

          you hold down the left mouse button and move the mouse, you 
          will paint the character onto the screen. 
           
              The placed character is in the color of the menu bar and 
          Font window. You can press <F4> to change the menu bar color, 
          but a placed character will not be affected. So let's paint the 

          placed character. 
           
              Press <F5> to enter Color mode. A "paint bucket" replaces 
          the arrow. You can pick up any color from the color bars. At 
          the left is the border/background color area. Move the bucket 
          to the far left edge of the Color window and click. The border 
          becomes whatever color is in the bucket. Point to the middle of 

          the screen color area and click again. The background becomes 
          whatever color is in the bucket. 
           
              You can move the bucket over characters on the screen and 
          click to paint the character. Press and hold the left mouse 
          button and move the mouse to paint the color all over the 
          place! 
           
              Now, let's modify a character. Press <F5> and <F3> until 
          the Font window appears. Use the cursor keys to move the font 











          cursor over one of the characters. Now you can point and click 
          in the Edit area to toggle pixels of the character off and on. 
           
              While Font and Color modes are extremely useful, you 
          probably do not want to do a lot of text this way. Press 
          <Shift-RETURN> to enter Text mode. Here you can type almost 
          exactly like you would with the C-64's text editor. The only 
          difference is that when you insert <Shift-BACKSPACE>, the right 

          end of the line "falls off" the screen. 
           
              To get back to the Font/Color screen, press <Shift-RETURN> 
          or <F1>. 
           
           
           THE MENUS 
           --------- 
              That was a quick tour. Now we need a list of the major 
          menus and all their functions. The hotkeys are capitalized on 
          the menu, and appear in <>'s in this text. To escape from any 
          of these menus, press <BACK ARROW> or click somewhere off the 
          menu. 
           FILE <F> 
           
            DISK <D> 
           
              Choose which disk drive is to be accessed by the program. 
          The current drive number has an asterisk beside it. 
           
            LOAD <L> 
           
              Expect a short wait as the program sorts out the useable 
          files on the disk. Simply scroll down and select the file you 
          want. Here is what each type of file contains: 
           
              *.FTS -- Font/Screen/Color data 
              *.TBS -- Screen/Color data 
              *.SOC -- Screen Object Collection 
              F.* -- Font 
           
              To escape from the scrolling menu, press <Q>. 
           
            SAVE <S> 
           
              Save FTS, TBS, SOC, or Font files. Choose the type of file, 

          then input the file name (no prefix or extension). The filename 

          of the most recently loaded file (sans prefix or extension) is 
          presented for you automatically. 
           
            PUT FTS <P> 
           
              Puts a copy of the current screen into a memory buffer, 
          which is a good idea if you are planning to make a mistake and 
          need to undo. 
           











            GET FTS <G> 
           
              Gets the copy of a screen in the memory buffer. Current 
          screen is lost. 
           
            SWAP FTS <W> 
           
              Swaps current screen with screen in the memory buffer. 
          Handy when moving cut portions from one screen to another. 
           
            FONT TO BUFF <F> 
           
              Copies the font line on which the font cursor resides to 
          the font buffer. 
           
            BUFF TO FONT <B> 
           
              Copies font buffer to the font line on which the cursor 
          resides. 
           
            NEW <N> 
           
              Wipes the screen, installs LOADSTAR standard font. 
           
            DISK COMS <C> 
           
              Gives you an input line where you can type in disk 
          commands. 
           
            EXIT <E> 
           
              The best possible way to leave the program. 
           
           EDIT <E> 
           
            BOX <B> 
           
              Allows you to set an area on the screen. The mouse arrow 
          turns into an open box, and a colored square appears near it. 
          As you move the mouse, the colored square moves to the nearest 
          text cell. Click (Fire/ RETURN/SPACE> to set the upper left 
          corner of the area you want to set. 
           
              The open box becomes four corners. Move the mouse down and 
          right to bracket the area you want to set, then click. You will 

          see "Box" on the right side of the menu bar, followed by the X 
          Y W H values. (Jot these down for use with .DREG in your DB+ 
          program.) The menu reopens for further commands. 
           NOTE: All other commands (except GET SOB and PASTE) must have 
          a box selected first. 
           
            COPY <C> 
           
              Copies the boxed area into a buffer. 











           
            CUT <U> 
           
              Copies the boxed area into a buffer, then clears the boxed 
          area of the screen. 
           
            REV CUT <R> 
           
              Copies the boxed area into a buffer, then fills the boxed 
          area with the reversed space (in the current menu bar color). 
           
              After Copy, cUt, or Rcut, you will see "Box Copied" on the 
          right side of the menu bar, and the menu reopens for further 
          commands. 
           
            PASTE <P> 
           
              Move the copied chunk of screen to any place on the screen 
          and click. 
           NOTE: Copy, cUt, or Rcut must be used before Paste will 
          operate. 
           
            FILL COLOR <F> 
           
              Fills the boxed area with the color of the menu bar. 
           
            TEXT FILL <T> 
           
              Fills the boxed area with the character under the font 
          cursor, in the color of the menu bar. 
           
            STORE SOB <S> 
           
              An SOB is a Screen OBject -- the area you Copied, cUt, or 
          Rcut. With this function, you can collect SOBs in a Screen 
          Object Collection, which you then can save and use in your 
          DotBASIC Plus program. 
           
            GET SOB <G> 
           
              This function can be performed without first setting a 
          boxed area. The SOBs in your collection (from Storing or from 
          loading an SOC file) can be viewed, attached to the mouse, 
          moved anywhere on the screen, and pasted there. 
           NOTE: A Screen Object Collection (either loaded or created 
          with STORE SOB) must be in memory for this function to work. 
           
              Use the Up/Down cursor keys to flip through the various 
          SOBs in the Collection. Press <RETURN> to select. You will be 
          asked to confirm. If you choose No, you return to the main 
          screen. 
           
              Choosing Yes connects the SOB to the cursor, which you can 
          move anywhere on the screen. Click to paste it. You will be 











          asked to confirm. If you choose No, you will be asked if you 
          want to continue. No takes you back to the main screen. Yes 
          lets you move the SOB around some more. 
           
           CONTROL <C> 
           
              All the controls are available from the main screen with 
          hot key presses. These are listed on the menu. You will 
          probably learn these key presses rather quickly. If not, here 
          they are on the menu! 
           
            MENU BAR <F1> 
           
              Toggles the menu bar off and on. 
           
            COLOR MODE <F2> 
           
              Switches between Normal, Extended Background, and 
          Multi-color text screen modes. The Color Window knows which 
          mode your are in, and shows the three (Multi-color) or four 
          (Extended Background) background colors. Click the bucket on 
          these areas to set these extra colors. 
           
            WINDOW TOGGLE <F3> 
           
              Toggles the Font/Color window on and off. 
           
            COLOR SELECT <F4> 
           
              Changes the color of the menu bar and all menus. A color 
          menu appears on the left edge of the screen. Use mouse, cursor, 

          or perss the standard <Ctrl-number> or <C=-number> combination 
          to choose your color. Press <BACKARROW> to exit with change. 
          until you like it! 
           
            FONT/COLOR TOGGLE <F5> 
           
              Toggles between the Font window and the Color window. 
           
            FONT FX <F6> 
           
              Turns on the Font Effects controls. The font cursor can be 
          moved with the cursor keys, or a character can be selected by 
          clicking on it in the font window. 
           
              CLEAR <C> 
           
                  Clears character. 
           
              SCROLL UP <U> 
           
                  Scrolls character up. 
           
              SCROLL LEFT <L> 











           
                  Scrolls character left. 
           
              FLIP HORIZONAL <H> 
           
                  Flips character horizontally. 
           
              TWIST <T> 
           
                  Twists character clockwise. 
           
              REVERSE <V> 
           
                  Reverses all pixels in 
                  character. 
           
              SCROLL DOWN <D> 
           
                  Scrolls character down. 
           
              SCROLL RIGHT <R> 
           
                  Scrolls character right. 
           
              FLIP VERTICALLY <F> 
           
                  Flips character vertically. 
           
              COPY <P> 
           
                  The arrow will move to the 
                  font area. Choose the 
                  character to which the current 
                  character will be copied and 
                  click. 
           
            Right Click to escape the Font FX 
              menu. 
           
            MOVE WINDOW <UP ARROW> 
           
              Moves Font/Color window to top or bottom of the screen. 
           
            TEXT MODE <Shift-RETURN> 
           
              In Text Mode, you can type to the screen almost exactly as 
          you would with the C-64's screen editor. Press <Ctrl-9> for 
          reverse, <Ctrl-0> for unreverse. Choose text colors whith 
          <CTRL> or <C=> and number keys. Insert can push the text off 
          the screen. 
           
              <Shift-RETURN> or <F1> exits Text Mode. 
           
           











           CLEVER THINGS TO DO 
           ------------------- 
              Let's make a screen with two shadowed boxes. Then we will 
          show you how to make each box a click-able button with rollover 

          effects. 
           
              So, press <F><N><1>, and get a new screen. We will use the 
          black background for the text. 
           
              First, make sure your menu bar color is white (press <F4> 
          until it is). Then bring the Font window up (<F3> and <F5>). 
          Click on the reverse space character. Right click to get rid of 

          it on your pointer. 
           
              Now press <E><B>, and define a box that includes the whole 
          screen. Press <T> to Text Fill the area. Wham! A white screen. 
          For perfectionists, press <F5> and make the border white as 
          well. 
           
              Change the menu bar color to blue, then press <E><B> again, 

          and define a box for your first button. Press <F> for Fill 
          Color. You have a box! Jot down the X/Y/W/H values displayed on 

          the right side of the menu bar. You will need this! 
           
              Do this again for the second box. 
           
              Back in the Color window, choose light gray, then paint a 
          shadow along the right and bottom of the two boxes. To finish 
          the job, press <Shift-RETURN> for Text mode, press <Ctrl-9> 
          then <Ctrl-7> for reverse blue characters. Move to your boxes 
          and give them some text. 
           
              Got it? Good! Time to save your work. You will want to 
          point to your work disk, so <F><D>, and choose the drive 
          number. Press <F><S><1> (to save an FTS file). Name the file 
          "myprog" -- or whatever you like. 
           
              One last thing to do, if you didn't do it already. Press 
          <E><B> and bracket your first box. On the right of the menu 
          bar, you will see 4 numbers. Jot these down. Then do the same 
          thing for the other box. 
           REFER TO THE .FTS DOTCOMMAND in the DotBIBLE to learn how to 
          use your designs in a DB+ program! 
           
           DMM 
           
